ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186252456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253002 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6eea42f5-3c3e-56b9-8e85-3dd91f7a6a1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186250835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186252628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186252739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186252739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253065 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "94357139-827d-5170-82dc-cc21ec831009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253272 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253272 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253272 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253272 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $(x+2)^{4}(x+5)(x-7)\\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253272 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f270ff5-bc17-53a5-be2e-3042a3d54393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253272 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)^{4}(x+5)(x-7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=7.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-2),\\,(-2,7),\\,(7,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-2)$ | $(-2,7)$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(x+2)^{4}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-7$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(7,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup {-2}\\cup [7,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253275 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253275 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253275 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253275 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253275 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a117df1a-bd81-5da6-88f9-198bb5436e1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253275 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+3x-28) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+7x-4x-28) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+7)-4(x+7)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-4)(x+7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x-4)(x+7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-7,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-7),\\,(-7,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(4,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}\\cup [4,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1191" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $A={x,y,z}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4dc7abe5-6826-51d9-82bd-d471faabcb9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186253546 #edb/ref 1074 "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/1d/1d7c11586a8fff5f63d46d936fd32c7b5fbfc33a406ddf268bfdd934fe787cdf.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{x},{y},{z},{x,y},{x,z},{y,z},{x,y,z}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254517 #edb/ref 1080 "{1,2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254517 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254517 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5eb7ccc0-36a1-5f64-909f-01aeeab6d0d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254596 #edb/ref 1080 "{-3,1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254596 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254596 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "66be3383-21b2-590e-a695-b35905404124" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254618 #edb/ref 1080 "∅" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254618 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254618 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "697eb32b-6e7b-5b33-b678-ff2fa69f0dbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186254917 #edb/ref 1080 